# Whether a smiley stands on its own, as ETI Eloquence 6.1 asks before it
# says one.
#
# The six smileys eng_tok_dict holds, :) and :( and their spellings with a
# nose and a wink, came from 6.1's token dictionary, and 6.1's arms for them
# say smiley face or sad face only when the smiley starts the text or a space
# is the character before it. Ours said them wherever the dictionary was
# asked, and the dictionary is asked again for what is left of a token once
# a word has been read off its front, so Local Disk (C:) was C smiley face.
# Anywhere else a smiley is its marks, as in 6.1: a colon's pause and a
# bracket that says nothing.

rule smiley_stands_alone takes 2 from ea_text.obj
  afresh
  local start bytes 8
  variable text_start word 756

  call get_parm addr start arg 1 -6
  call fence 0 sym eti_null
  call lpta_loadp addr start
  call setscan_l 1
  if answer
    give up
  end
  plant scan after_space
  call lpta_loadp addr text_start
  call test_ptr
  if answer
    backtrack
  end
  match

place after_space
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym eti_s_1b90d7
  if answer
    give up
  end
  match
end

# A lone Russian letter said by its Russian name. English names a letter out
# of eng_char_name, which knows the bytes Russian's letters took as the
# accented letters they were -- в alone was a circumflex -- so the call
# that reached it reaches this, which lays the letter's own name down in
# its place for a Russian letter, and a symbol's Russian name for a symbol,
# and hands any other character on. The
# name is letters, read back as a word; a vowel's name is the vowel.
rule ru_char_name takes 3 from ea_text.obj
  afresh
  call ZZfence_null
  call lpta_loadp arg 1                 # а: а
  call setscan_r 1
  if answer is 0
    call test_string_s 1 1 sym ru_lt_61
    if answer is 0
      call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
